I have repeatedly asked you for an operational definition
of microscopic angular momentum covering spin one half
systems, and every time I ask you quote the Stern
Gerlach experiment, in patronizing tones.

If you have an operational definition of angular momentum,
come on out with it.

Describing the world in terms of operations is like
describing the world in terms of the two dimensional images
formed on our retina.  It does not work, in some cases it
is vastly complex, and it is false.

The concepts of science have observable consequences, and
they are themselves real things, or descriptions of real
things.  They are not their own consequences, and if we
attempt to describe them in terms of their consequences,
our descriptions sometimes become hideously convoluted,
obscure misleading and inaccurate.

Some things can be well expressed in operational terms,
for example linear momentum.  Some things cannot.

