kwatson@netcom.com (Kennita Watson) writes:

> I would love to hear some speculation on what some of these [poison
> pill] forms might be -- I haven't been able to think of anything
> more subtle than dynamiting the place.

One which until recently worked in Australia (against anyone *but* the
federal tax office sadly) was to simply have a large tax debt. Because
the federal taxman got first bite on liquidation/bankruptcy everyone
else (including state tax) had a negative motivation to close you
down: if they were getting *anything* from you they were probably
better off than they would have been in a wind-up. 

I don't know if you can get them in the US, but try looking for any book
by Peter Clyne [1] and in particular his books on bankruptcy[2]. Until
his death Clyne was Australia's best known tax dodger and happiest
bankrupt. While some of the methods mentioned only work under
Australian law and some of it is now out of date, his books are very
amusing to read and still contain valuable ideas.

Major

[1] Except the book on meetings. While it is probably the most
    readable introduction to the law of meetings in existance, it
    bears no relation to the art of judgement proofing.

[2] One of the rituals of bankruptcy is the public examination, Clyne
    warns you must never be caught lying in such an examination. In
    his own examination he was asked:
    "Are you engaged in any income-producing activities?"
    "yes"
    "what are you doing?"
    "writing a book"
    "what is the title of this book"
    "how to make a profit by going bankrupt"


