Date: Wed, 6 Oct 93 2:25:39 PDT
From: tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May)
Subject: A Modest Proposal on the Writing of Satires

This business of satires, dry humor, put-ons, and fat in the brain is
an interesting theme.

As an occasional satirist--I know you all are shocked, simply
shocked--one of the things I do when reading dry posts about fat
intake and intelligence, for example, is to look for an angle to make
some punks, some Swiftian satires, or to poke holes in the argument by
finding counterexamples.

When I read the Brooks piece, I didn't spot the phony names--I skimmed
too fast to note their exact pronunciation when spoken aloud, but I
did think of counterexamples which I was mentally preparing to
assemble into a satirical attack on the piece:

- concentration camp survivors like Viktor Frankl, Elie Wiesel, and
many others who had presumably been taked to the point of "brain
self-digestion" and yet retained their intelligenge (once fattened up
again).

- low body-fat people like Perry

Anyway, these obvious contradictions told me the thesis of the article
was suspect (although malnourished infants can, I understand, remain
retarded permanently...this was not the point of the "brain
self-degestion" piece, though).

Once suspicious, it fell into place. The "brain is mostly fat anyway"
point is similar to the outrageous comments many put-ons contain.

I guess I should have played along by noting Perry's dullness as his
brain digested itself--after all, Perry created the self-digestifying
list, didn't he? 

The point is this: writing satires is often good innoculation against
bogosity, something I'm sure Robert Anton Wilson understands very
fnord well. And I'm sure master satirist David Victor de Transend
does, too, though this particular case may have been more subtle than
is desirable for a much-skimmed, seldom-read deeply list.

Try writing satires, as many of you do already, and you may find the
world looking a little different. It worked for Ambrose Bierce, G. B.
Shaw, R. A. Wilson, and a host of others; it may work for you. An
Extropian thing to do, methinks.

--Tim May


