Date: Wed, 6 Oct 1993 11:46:28 +0100 (BST)
From: Charlie Stross <charless@sco.com>
Subject: books online

pmetzger@lehman.com (Perry E. Metzger) writes:

>I've been scanning through a CD-ROM put out by Clarinet containing all
>(ALL!) the things nominated last year for Hugo and Nebula awards (six
>novels, lots of short fiction, artwork, etc) plus lots of other
>goodies (a hypertext annotated version of A Fire Upon the Deep is just
>one of them). Amazing. Only $28 for $230 in books, and the authors get
>better royalties this way. Admittedly I need a giant font to read
>books online without a headache, but so what -- I have a 19 inch
>monitor and can spare the bits. Electronic books are here! Wow!

Indeed.

This has been a favourite topic of mine -- in other forums -- for
some years now. "Imminent death of publishing marketing chain
predicted". It's not happened yet, but ...

You've all seen 1-hour photo labs in shops. Imagine a 1-minute book
lab in a bookshop. You dial in your selection. The machine connects to
the publishers' server, transfers some digital cash tokens, and
downloads the encrypted postscript image of a book. (Yes, yes, there
are copyright issues -- but they're not insuperable. Printing machines
don't run away in a hurry.) The printer then zaps out a 600dpi image,
duplex, 2-up on custom paper, while a thermal transfer printer
prepares the hot-foil color cover. Both printers feed into a
collating/binding machine, and a minute later a fresh paperback pops
out of a slot, still hot from the fuser.

SF? No. This kind of kit costs about $50,000 today -- and the price is
dropping. 

Of course, this isn't going to replace the offset litho for large
runs just yet. For one thing, it's more expensive. For another,
economies of scale set in when you're printing a best-seller.

What it will hit:

*       Specialist books. No more big print runs; they'll be 
        produced on demand. See it in a catalogue, order it --
        ten minutes later, it's yours.

*       Out-of-print books. Ditto.

*       Specialist bookshops. For obvious reasons, the value of
        carrying a big stocklist will drop.

*       Distributors. Warehousing and distribution will cease
        to be relevant except for high-volume mass-market 
        batches.

Who it will benefit:

*       Big bookseller chains who can afford the printers.

*       Big publishers with publicity departments. Book circulation
        will be a function of publicity and promotion.

*       'Cult' books. Word-of-mouth sales will no longer be blocked
        by poor distribution or marketing by the publisher; expect
        unexpected hits, the decay of the 'formula' bestseller
        genre.

*       Vanity publishing; anyone will be able to produce a book
        and get it distributed. With sufficient advertising and an
        acceptable product, it will sell.

On-line publishing will have to wait for the PDA to (a) catch on
(costs of under $300 a unit, as predicted in Byte this month, bode
well for it), and (b) for the screen resolution to improve to, say,
1024x1280 pixels in monochrome in a 4" x 6" screen -- comparable to
paper.

(Any other comments? ;-)



-- Charlie
